  "answer": "The study confirmed five settings above the 0.5 bar, each on a different scene, which meets the goal: S1 (048b974e, 0.6x, \u22121.5 s), S6 (07981e6a, 1.4x, \u22120.5 s), S10 (0caa8f1a, 1.0x, +1.5 s), S14 (0d4893f5, 1.2x, +1.0 s) and S20 (18d28973, 0.8x, 0 s). Every repeat at these five failed, but with so few repeats the 90% ranges only show the failure rate is above one half. However, these settings don't answer the question that was asked. The triage of the videos and logs shows that no failure involved the pedestrian. In every case the policy ignored a RIGHT route command and drifted or turned left. It then hit a parked or oncoming car, a car in the next lane, or ran onto a median. These five scenes also failed at very different speeds and step-out times, so the failures come from the scene and the policy's route-following, not from the speed\u2013timing combination. The study found no setting where speed and pedestrian timing together cause a pedestrian collision. S1, S10 and S20 were also flagged as possible simulator artifacts because the ego ended far from the recorded path, but the triage puts these on the policy: the drift started while the camera view was still clean.",
